  "ai_language_tip": "Speak clear standard Finnish (fi); Swedish (sv) is the second national language and the majority language in Ostrobothnian coast towns and Åland, so switch if the owner answers in Swedish; English is very widely spoken, especially under 50.",
  "english_ok": "yes — Very high English proficiency; elderly rural staff and some public services prefer Finnish or Swedish.",
  "politeness": "Brief and neutral; 'te' (formal you) is rarely needed, polite neutral forms suffice; open with 'Hyvää päivää' or 'Moi' and say whom you call for; close with 'Kiitos, hei hei'. Silence on the line is normal, not rudeness.",
  "name_tip": "First name + surname; surname alone is fine for a booking ('nimellä Virtanen'); Finnish adds case endings to names in speech (Virtaselle), so spell the base form letter by letter.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 04x / 050 + 6–8 digits (e.g. 040 123 4567), Helsinki landline 09, +358; SMS confirmations normal.",
  "time_tip": "TRAP: 'puoli kahdeksan' = 7:30 (half to eight); 'varttia vaille kahdeksan' = 7:45; times written with a dot ('klo 19.30'); read back in 24h.",
  "number_tip": "Numbers take case endings in speech ('neljälle' = for four, 'kahdeksalta' = at eight); 'as. 12' is a flat number; read phone numbers in groups.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch 11:00–12:30 (Finns lunch early), dinner peak 17:30–19:30, calls after 19:00, weekends for offices, and July holiday season plus Juhannus (Midsummer) week when many firms close.",
  "closed_days": "Shops may open on Sundays; public holidays close most businesses (e.g. Loppiainen 6 January, Good Friday, Easter Monday, Vappu 1 May, Ascension, Juhannus eve and day, 6 December, 24–26 December); July is the main holiday month.",
